<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Is Cameron Carr Paid on the Rookie Scale?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Carr is paid <strong>$3.32m</strong> because the rookie scale fixes the figure for the 24th pick, and a player entering the league has no say in it.</p>
<ul>
<li>The 24th pick carries a set salary that rises each season, decided before he plays.</li>
<li>Only the first two seasons are guaranteed, worth <strong>$6.8m</strong> of the <strong>$16.82m</strong>.</li>
<li>He is 6’5″ and 175 pounds, which is light for a guard at this level.</li>
<li>Both later seasons are team options, which is standard on every rookie scale contract.</li>
<li>He joins a roster whose top three salaries take up almost <strong>$121m</strong>, so the minutes have to be earned.</li>
</ul>
<p>His four-year contract is worth about what Dončić earns in four months.</p>

